Input: {"question": "When was Egg Rock Light constructed?", "original_source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d00", "start": 0, "end": 78, "text": "Egg Rock Light (Maine) Egg Rock Light is a lighthouse on Frenchman Bay, Maine."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d00", "start": 79, "end": 224, "text": "Built in 1875, it is one of coastal Maine's architecturally unique lighthouses, with a square tower projecting through the square keeper's house."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d00", "start": 225, "end": 374, "text": "Located on Egg Rock, midway between Mount Desert Island and the Schoodic Peninsula, it is an active aid to navigation, flashing red every 40 seconds."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d00", "start": 375, "end": 474, "text": "The light was listed on the National Register of Historic Places as Egg Rock Light Station in 1988."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d00", "start": 475, "end": 602, "text": "The Egg Rock Light Station consists of two buildings, a combination light tower and keeper's house, and a fog station building."}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d01", "start": 0, "end": 26, "text": "Maine that can be toured ."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d01", "start": 27, "end": 131, "text": "Standing 54 feet tall , the white brick and cast-iron light first shone in 1897 and still shines today ."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d01", "start": 132, "end": 189, "text": "The former assistant keeper ’ s bedroom is now a museum ."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d01", "start": 190, "end": 334, "text": "Portland Breakwater | South Portland Also called Bug Light , this 26-foot , cast-iron lighthouse is located at the entrance to Portland Harbor ."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d01", "start": 335, "end": 400, "text": "The lighthouse isn ’ t available to visit , but the grounds are ."}, "D2S6": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d01", "start": 401, "end": 453, "text": "You just park at Bug Light Park and enjoy yourself ."}, "D2S7": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d01", "start": 454, "end": 538, "text": "The lighthouse was constructed in 1875 and decommissioned in 1943 , then reactivated"}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d02", "start": 0, "end": 16, "text": "Wildlife Refuge."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d02", "start": 17, "end": 100, "text": "Accessibility: The lighthouse is not open to the public and is best viewed by boat."}, "D3S3": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d02", "start": 101, "end": 552, "text": "Station established: 1875 Present lighthouse built: 1920 Automated: 1976 Construction material: Brick, wood Other buildings still standing: 1904 fog signal building, oil house, boathouse, generator house Height of tower: 40 feet Height of focal plane: 64 feet Original optic: Fifth-order Fresnel lens Present optic: VRB-25 (below) Characteristic: Flashing red every five seconds Fog signal: Two blasts every 30 seconds Previous lighthouse: Bear Island"}, "D3S4": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d02", "start": 554, "end": 669, "text": "In 1920, the present Egg Rock Light was constructed to replace the original structure, which had served since 1875."}, "D3S5": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d02", "start": 670, "end": 786, "text": "The new lighthouse was built using brick and wood, and it stands 40 feet tall, with a focal plane height of 64 feet."}, "D3S6": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d02", "start": 787, "end": 960, "text": "The lighthouse was automated in 1976, and several other buildings, including a fog signal building from 1904, an oil house, a boathouse, and a generator house, remain on the"}, "D4S1": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d03", "start": 0, "end": 21, "text": "Frenchman Bay, Maine."}, "D4S2": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d03", "start": 22, "end": 167, "text": "Built in 1920, it is one of coastal Maine's architecturally unique lighthouses, with a square tower projecting through the square keeper's house."}, "D4S3": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d03", "start": 168, "end": 317, "text": "Located on Egg Rock, midway between Mount Desert Island and the Schoodic Peninsula, it is an active aid to navigation, flashing red every 40 seconds."}, "D4S4": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d03", "start": 318, "end": 417, "text": "The light was listed on the National Register of Historic Places as Egg Rock Light Station in 1988."}, "D4S5": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d03", "start": 418, "end": 521, "text": "The Egg Rock Light Station consists of two buildings, a combination light tower and keeper's house, and"}, "D5S1": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d04", "start": 0, "end": 131, "text": "Egg Rock Light (Massachusetts) Egg Rock Light was first established in 1856 after a schooner went down with the loss of five lives."}, "D5S2": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d04", "start": 132, "end": 233, "text": "The original structure was a lantern on top of a stone dwelling built from granite cut on the island."}, "D5S3": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0193-013750095677:d04", "start": 234, "end": 427, "text": "After the wreck of the schooner \"Shark\" the characteristic was changed to fixed red because the captain of the \"Shark\" was said to have confused it with Long 